.TH NULL 4 "February 2026" "Substrate Kernel" "Device Drivers Manual"
.SH NAME
null \- the null device
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /dev/null
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B null
device acts as a data sink and source of end-of-file indications.
.PP
Writing to the
.B null
device discards all data. The write operation always succeeds and returns the count of bytes written.
.PP
Reading from the
.B null
device always returns 0 (end-of-file).
.PP
Seeking on the
.B null
device is supported and updating the file offset succeeds, but has no effect on subsequent reads or writes.
.PP
The device supports
.BR poll (2)
and always reports
.B POLLOUT
(writable). It does not report
.B POLLIN
(readable) because end-of-file is not considered data availability in this implementation.
.SH FILES
.B /dev/null
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B ENOTTY
The device does not support any ioctl operations.
.TP
.B EINVAL
Mapping the device via
.BR mmap (2)
is not supported.
.SH EXAMPLES
Discard standard output of a command:
.PP
.nf
    $ echo "hi" > /dev/null
.fi
.PP
Read EOF immediately:
.PP
.nf
    $ cat /dev/null
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR zero (4),
.BR full (4)
.SH HISTORY
The
.B null
device appeared in Version 7 AT&T UNIX.
.SH BUGS
The implementation does not support
.BR kqueue (2)
filters.
